.TH PSMORE 1 "2026-08-02" "psmore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
psmore \- relationship-first process diagnostics for macOS and Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B psmore
.RI [ QUERY ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B psmore
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGUMENTS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B psmore
is an interactive process tree and a scriptable diagnostics workbench. It keeps
parent, child, sibling, service-subtree, command-line, resource, network, file,
and lifecycle evidence in one model. The default mode opens a terminal UI;
subcommands provide stable tables and versioned JSON for SSH sessions, incident
records, and health gates.
.PP
Collection is read-only unless the user explicitly invokes a process action in
the TUI. Visibility is limited by operating-system permissions. Missing evidence
is reported as incomplete or inconclusive rather than treated as proof that a
condition is absent.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check QUERY
Evaluate whether a process query has any or no matches. The collector process
and its contribution to ancestor subtree metrics are excluded from the gate.
Use
.B --wait
to retry until a condition converges and
.B --stable
to require consecutive passing evaluations.
.TP
.B inspect PID
Inspect one process, its runtime context, threads, sockets, files, limits, and
security evidence.
.TP
.B port PORT
Find processes and socket references associated with a local TCP or UDP port.
.TP
.B listen
Inventory listening sockets, bind exposure, owners, and namespaces.
.TP
.B net
Search listening, connected, peer, and Unix socket evidence.
.TP
.B tree PID
Print a stable ancestor chain and nested descendants for one process.
.TP
.B watch
Stream starts, exits, PID reuse, reparenting, and query membership changes.
.TP
.B trace PID
Sample process and service-subtree CPU, memory, and I/O over time.
.TP
.B run [OPTIONS] -- COMMAND [ARGUMENTS]
Launch COMMAND and profile every observable process in its descendant tree.
The command inherits standard input, output, and error; the final profile is
written to the diagnostic standard error stream so command output remains safe
for pipelines. The psmore exit status mirrors COMMAND. Use
.B --linger-ms
to bound post-exit descendant observation. Use
.B --output
to atomically write an unambiguous private JSON report while preserving command
standard error.
.TP
.B deleted
Find deleted files that remain open and estimate reclaimable space.
.TP
.B file PATH
Find processes executing, mapping, opening, or using PATH as their current or
root directory. Existing targets are also matched by device and inode. Use
.B --recursive
to include every descendant of a directory or mount point; use
.B --expect none
as a conservative pre-replacement or pre-unmount safety gate.
.TP
.B fd
Rank file-descriptor pressure and compare counts with known process limits.
.TP
.B top
Rank process or complete subtree CPU, memory, read, and write hotspots.
.TP
.B oom
On Linux, correlate OOM selection priority with memory, swap, PSI, cgroup, and
kernel OOM evidence.
.TP
.B doctor
Run conservative host and process triage. With
.BR --deep ,
also scan network exposure, FD pressure, deleted-open files, and Linux OOM/PSI.
.TP
.B diff BEFORE AFTER
Compare two process snapshots or two host doctor reports. Doctor reports may use
.B --fail-on regression
as an automation gate.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Write a completion script to standard output.
.SH PROCESS QUERY
Plain terms search names and command context case-insensitively. Structured
fields include
.BR name: ,
.BR cmd: ,
.BR path: ,
.BR user: ,
.BR state: ,
.BR pid: ,
and
.BR ppid: .
Numeric conditions include
.BR cpu> ,
.BR mem>= ,
.BR read> ,
.BR write> ,
.BR age> ,
.BR children>= ,
.BR tree.cpu> ,
.BR tree.mem> ,
and
.BR tree.procs>= .
Prefix a condition with
.B !
to exclude it. Memory and I/O units accept k, m, g, and t; age accepts s, m, h,
and d.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -q , " --query " QUERY
Apply a process query.
.TP
.B --table
Write a stable human-readable table and exit.
.TP
.B --json
Write a versioned machine-readable JSON document and exit.
.TP
.B --redact
Mask common secret flags, assignments, headers, URLs, and query values in
command lines. Always review output before sharing it.
.TP
.BI --sample-ms " MILLISECONDS"
Set the resource sampling interval where supported.
.TP
.BI --wait " DURATION"
For
.BR check ,
retry until the policy passes or the timeout expires. Durations accept ms, s,
m, or h and must be at least as long as the sampling interval.
.TP
.BI --interval-ms " MILLISECONDS"
For check, set the evaluation cadence while waiting. For run, set the command
subtree sampling interval.
.TP
.BI --linger-ms " MILLISECONDS"
For run, continue observing known descendants for this many milliseconds after
the launched command exits. The default is 1000; the accepted range is 0 to
60000.
.TP
.BI --output " FILE"
For run, atomically write a mode-0600 JSON report without mixing it with command
standard error. Existing files are refused unless
.B --force
is also specified.
.TP
.BI --stable " COUNT"
For
.BR check ,
require COUNT consecutive passing evaluations. A failing evaluation resets the
consecutive count.
.TP
.B --recursive
For
.BR file ,
match the resolved path and all descendants using component-aware path
comparison.
.TP
.BI --limit " COUNT|all"
Limit returned evidence rows for commands that support it. Policy evaluation is
performed before truncation.
.TP
.BI --expect " none|any"
Apply a policy to all matches. Confirmed violations exit 3. A zero-match result
with incomplete collection is inconclusive and exits 1.
.TP
.B --quiet
Suppress normal output for a command with an explicit policy, using only the
exit status.
.TP
.B --no-tips
Suppress startup guidance for this TUI run without changing saved preferences.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show global or command-specific help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the psmore version.
.SH TUI KEYS
.TP
.BR Up / Down , " j / k"
Move the selection. The viewport follows the selected row.
.TP
.BR Left / Right , " h / l"
Reveal parent context or expand/collapse the selected process subtree.
.TP
.B /
Search and locate matching processes. Enter accepts the located result; normal
navigation leaves locate mode while retaining the result context.
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume live refresh.
.TP
.BR n ,
.BR d ,
.BR b ,
and
.B i
Open network, deep inspection, baseline/diff, or process detail workspaces.
.TP
.BR ?
Open the field guide. Within guidance,
.B T
toggles future tips and
.B D
disables startup cards.
.TP
.BR q
Close the current panel or quit from the main process tree.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The command completed and any requested policy passed.
.TP
.B 1
Collection or runtime failed, or evidence was insufficient for a conclusive
policy result.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage, input schema, or unsafe output selection was invalid.
.TP
.B 3
A confirmed expectation, severity threshold, or regression policy failed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/Library/Application Support/psmore/ui-state.json
Default macOS TUI preference state.
.TP
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-~/.config}/psmore/ui-state.json
Default Linux TUI preference state.
.TP
.I PREFIX/share/psmore/uninstall.sh
Uninstaller for a binary-package installation. It does not remove TUI state or
diagnostic reports.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PSMORE_CONFIG_DIR
Override the directory used for private TUI preference state.
.TP
.B PSMORE_PREFIX
Override the binary package install prefix. The default is
.IR ~/.local .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Select the Linux user configuration root when
.B PSMORE_CONFIG_DIR
is unset.
.SH PRIVACY AND SAFETY
Process arguments, paths, usernames, hostnames, sockets, and files may contain
sensitive data. JSON exports are created privately when written through psmore,
but redaction is best effort. Review reports before sharing them. Run with only
the privileges needed for the incident; higher privileges increase visibility
and the impact of explicit process actions.
.PP
The
.B file
command is read-only. Linux reads executable, cwd, root, descriptor, and mapping
evidence from
.IR /proc ;
macOS performs one global
.BR lsof (8)
scan. psmore and its collection helper are excluded. Incomplete visibility is
reported explicitly and never used to prove that a path has no users.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ps (1),
.BR lsof (8),
.BR top (1)
